// t-fntyp-01: merged, covariant reference types let `*r <- v` store an
// ill-typed value; the JIT panics.
//
// merge_inner (graphix-types/src/typ/normalize.rs:557) merges two references'
// targets, so the select below is typed `&[i64, string]` (`let z: i64 = r`
// reports exactly that), a reference to a variable that exists nowhere. A
// written `[&i64, &string]` normalizes the same way. ConnectDeref's check
// (graphix-compiler/src/node/mod.rs:2361) then accepts `*r <- "s"`, which
// lands in `x: i64`.
//
// command: graphix --check design/review-2026-10-05/repro/t-fntyp-01.gx
//          graphix --no-cache design/review-2026-10-05/repro/t-fntyp-01.gx
//          graphix --no-cache --no-fusion design/review-2026-10-05/repro/t-fntyp-01.gx
//
// expected: the check refuses `*r <- "s"`: `r` may point at `x: i64`.
//
// observed: --check exits 0. The fused run panics and the runtime dies:
//   panicked at graphix-compiler/src/fusion/kernel.rs:243:13:
//   kernel param `x`: runtime String("s") does not match the compiled Scalar(I64) slot
//   Error: runtime did not respond  (exit 1)
// --no-fusion prints 2, then `x + 1` over x = "s" logs an arith error.
//
// Not merging is not enough on its own. With `let y = [1]` and
// `*r <- [2, 3]` the two targets do not merge (r is typed
// `[&i64, &Array<i64>]`), yet the check still passes, because ConnectDeref
// accepts a write when ANY member of r's type contains `&typeof(v)`. The
// fused run panics the same way (runtime Array([2, 3]) in `x`), and
// --no-fusion prints 2 then [3, 3]: the i64 `x` holds an array.
let x = 1;
let y = "a";
let c = true;
let r = select c { true => &x, false => &y };
*r <- "s";
sys::exit(sys::time::after_idle(duration:300.ms, 0));
x + 1
